corpus/documents/emails/residue_w36.md:3:Assuming we go ahead with the course I recommended, the birds would be on amoxicillin in the water for five days. That knocks back the infection, but amoxicillin carries an egg withdrawal: eggs laid during dosing, and for five days after the last dose, can't go into the food supply at all.
corpus/documents/emails/residue_w36.md:5:This is different from the salmonella side of things, and I want to be clear about it because it trips people up — you can't process your way out of a drug residue. Breaking or pasteurizing doesn't remove it; the residue is still there and still over the limit. So there's no diverting this volume to breaking stock as a way to salvage it. For the whole withdrawal window, House 5's eggs have to come off every account and be destroyed — not sold as table eggs, not sent to a breaker, kept out of the human food chain entirely.
corpus/documents/emails/residue_w36.md:7:Practically: the withdrawal runs until five days after the last dose, so I'd pull and destroy House 5's production from the start of treatment through that point — call it about ten days of House 5 volume. I know that's real money gone and Brett won't like it, but selling or diverting eggs through a drug withdrawal isn't something I can put my name to.
corpus/documents/emails/mite_w14.md:11:Options if you want to act: a systemic acaricide (fluralaner in water) is the most reliable knockdown but carries an egg withdrawal period; or a more aggressive heat/IPM cleanout of the harborage. Happy to talk it through and write the script if you want to go that route. Untreated, these populations roughly double every couple weeks in this temp range.

